extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Bar"
	- pagedescription = "Bar charts in Tablechart: bars laid sideways, so long names fit on one line."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Bar
				p.lead Bars laid sideways, for long names.

	+section("Bar chart")
		p Each row of the table is a bar. The number is shown after it. The names are shown to the left of the bars, where a long name has more room than under a column. If a name is still too long, it wraps onto a second line. See #[a(href='#long-names') Long names].

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="bar")
				figcaption
					b Juice pressed by variety
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Bramley seedling', '31.4'], ['Cox’s orange pippin', '27.8'], ['Egremont russet', '18.2'], ['Discovery', '12.6'], ['Worcester pearmain', '9.1']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Numbers inside the bars")
		p Add #[code data-chart-labels="inside"] to show each number inside its bar, at the end. To align it at the start of the bar or in the middle instead, see #[a(href=url('/styling/text.html')) Text]. If a bar is too short for its number, the number is shown after it.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="bar" data-chart-labels="inside")
					table
						tbody
							each row in [['Bramley seedling', '31.4'], ['Cox’s orange pippin', '27.8'], ['Egremont russet', '18.2'], ['Discovery', '12.6'], ['Worcester pearmain', '9.1']]
								tr
									th= row[0]
									td= row[1]

			+codeblock('HTML')
				figure(data-chart="bar" data-chart-labels="inside")

	+section("Long names")
		p A name beside a bar takes at most 40% of the width of the chart. A longer name wraps onto more lines, and the chart gets taller if it needs to. To change the width, set #[code --tablechart-name-max], such as #[code 30%].
		p A long word is hyphenated if the page has a #[code lang] attribute, such as #[code lang="en"].
		p Or give the row a short name for the chart with #[code data-short], such as #[code &lt;th data-short="Cox"&gt;Cox’s Orange Pippin&lt;/th&gt;]. The table, and so a screen reader, keeps the full name. See #[a(href=url('/types/line.html#many-rows')) Many rows].

	+section("Below zero")
		p A bar with a value below zero goes to the left of the zero line, with its number before it.

		+chart(null, '7-8,10')
			figure(data-chart="bar")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6'], ['Feb', '28.7'], ['Mar', '-11.7'], ['Apr', '-36.3'], ['May', '19.8'], ['Jun', '-4.1']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p With several value columns, each bar of a group goes left or right on its own.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="bar")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th 2024
							th 2025
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6', '-2.6'], ['Feb', '28.7', '-12.2'], ['Mar', '-11.7', '12.0'], ['Apr', '-36.3', '26.8'], ['May', '19.8', '-6.9'], ['Jun', '-4.1', '7.5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Grouped bars")
		p If the table has more than one value column, each row is a group, with one bar for each value column. The column headers are the names in the legend under the chart.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="bar")
				figcaption
					b Apples sold by variety
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th 2022
							th 2026
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '118', '131', false], ['Granny Johnson', '12', '45', 'granny'], ['Jonagold', '140', '135', false], ['Braeburn', '109', '121', false]]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Stacked bars")
		p Add #[code data-chart-stack] to the figure. The values of a row are shown one after the other in one bar, the first value column on the left. Values below zero are stacked to the left of the zero line. The total of the row is after the bar.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="bar" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Apples sold
					br
					span thousands
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Farm shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '64', '38'], ['Granny Johnson', '12', '21'], ['Jonagold', '71', '45'], ['Braeburn', '52', '30']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p A number is shown inside its part of the bar if it fits. If not, it is left out. If you point at the row, or tap it, a tooltip shows the numbers of that row.
		p Below zero, the parts of a stack go left from the zero line. The total is the sum of all parts, after the bar, or before it if the whole stack is below zero.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="bar" data-chart-stack)
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Month
							th Shop
							th Market
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12', '-4'], ['Feb', '8', '-9'], ['Mar', '-6', '3'], ['Apr', '-14', '-7'], ['May', '4', '-2'], ['Jun', '10', '5']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

	+section("Scale break")
		p If one bar is much longer than the others, a break in the scale shows part of the scale smaller, so the other bars have room. See #[a(href=url('/features/scale-break.html')) Scale break].

		+chart(null, '10')
			figure(data-chart="bar")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Bramley seedling', '140'], ['Cox’s orange pippin', '15'], ['Egremont russet', '14'], ['Discovery', '12']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="axis-break" data-from="20")

	+section("Axis and gridlines")
		p By default there is no value axis: the numbers on the bars show the values. To add numbers along the axis, under the bars, add #[code data-chart-axis]. To add a light line at each of those numbers, add #[code data-chart-grid]. You can add one of them, or both. To add them to every chart, set the #[code axis] and #[code grid] options to #[code true].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="bar" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption
					b Juice pressed by variety
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Bramley seedling', '31.4'], ['Cox’s orange pippin', '27.8'], ['Egremont russet', '18.2'], ['Discovery', '12.6'], ['Worcester pearmain', '9.1']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a chart has an axis or gridlines, its scale ends on a number of the axis. To set the scale yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale], such as #[code data-chart-scale="0 100"]. To change how the axis and gridlines look, see #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Next")
		p For short names, a #[a(href=url('/types/column.html')) column chart] uses less height.
